# Status bar

Status Bar summarizes the current health of a service and presents its historical status as a compact series of segments. Each segment exposes its label and incident details on hover or keyboard focus. Pass a `descriptions` map to customize fallback copy for each status without repeating it on every item. Use the `tooltip` snippet for a completely custom layout; it receives `item`, `status`, `statusLabel`, `description`, and `index`.

<DocsExample title="Status Bar" source={statusBarExampleSource} htmlSource={statusBarExampleHtmlSource}><StatusBarExample /></DocsExample>

## Interactive live service

Switch the service off to create an ongoing outage, then switch it back on to close the outage. Add latency events to see how they affect the current interval.

<DocsExample title="Interactive live status timeline" source={statusBarInteractiveExampleSource} htmlSource={statusBarInteractiveExampleHtmlSource}><StatusBarInteractiveExample /></DocsExample>

## Data models

Pass pre-aggregated `items` without `interval` to render one segment per item. For event streams, set `interval` and `total`; Status Bar creates time buckets, groups overlapping events, and uses the most severe status in each bucket.

- `getEvent` adapts application records to `time`/`endTime`, `status`, and `label`. Records with `online: true` or `online: false` mark state changes that continue until the next change.
- `getIntervalColor` can return a custom color for a generated bucket.
- `formatInterval` controls generated labels.
- `defaultStatus` fills intervals without events.
- `startDate` fixes the timeline anchor; without it, the current interval advances automatically.

The custom `tooltip` snippet also receives the bucket's `events` and `occurrences`.
